## Score bands

Each dimension is graded fails / meets / exceeds. `overall_pass` is true only when every dimension is at least meets; a single fails makes the rubric fail.

## Consent (rule r1)

- fails: any tool runs without a user prompt, or consent persists across sessions
- meets: first call per tool prompts in a distinguishable surface; decision cached per session at most
- exceeds: meets, plus per-call prompts for write tools and a visible consent ledger in the UI
- evidence: <consent-flow doc, screenshots, code path>
- consent_grade: `<consent_grade>`

## Auth (rule r2)

- n/a: scope local_desktop with stdio only; record meets with note "local, host boundary"
- fails: any remote endpoint reachable without OAuth 2.1 + PKCE (api_key or anonymous)
- meets: OAuth 2.1 with PKCE enforced on every remote endpoint; tokens scoped and expiring
- exceeds: meets, plus per-tool scopes and token audience bound to the resource server URL
- evidence: <authorization server, scope list, negative test>
- auth_grade: `<auth_grade>`

## Injection defence (rules r3, r5)

- fails: tool output or tool descriptions inlined raw into the next prompt, or tools from one server able to invoke another server's tools implicitly
- meets: tool output treated as data (quoted, flagged for suspicious tokens); tool descriptions rendered in a separate surface; per-server isolation with no shared session or credentials
- exceeds: meets, plus automated injection probes in CI against every server
- evidence: <bridge code review, probe results>
- injection_grade: `<injection_grade>`

## Capability attestation (rule r4)

- fails: client accepts methods the server did not declare in `initialize`
- meets: client rejects undeclared methods and logs the attempt
- exceeds: meets, plus declared capabilities pinned in config and diffed on every connect
- evidence: <client code path, log sample>
- folded into: injection_grade (take the lower of the two)

## Audit and identity (rules r6, r7)

- fails: any tool call missing from the log, or servers loaded outside an allow-list
- meets: every call logged with server_id, tool_name, caller_session, arguments_hash, outcome, timestamp; log stored outside the MCP host; allow-list enforced with signed manifest or pinned cert
- exceeds: meets, plus tamper-evident log (append-only, hashed) and alerting on identity mismatch
- evidence: <log sample, allow-list file, verification code>
- audit_grade: `<audit_grade>`

